          "title": "Lesson 1.1: The Addition and Multiplication Principles",
          "summary": "Count choices with “or” (add) and “and” (multiply), and combine the two.",
          "body": "## Two ways to count without listing\n\nCounting every possibility one by one is slow and easy to get wrong. Two simple principles let you count large sets quickly: the **Addition Principle** and the **Multiplication Principle**.\n\n## The Addition Principle (“or”)\n\nUse it when you choose **exactly one** item from several groups that have nothing in common. Such choices are **mutually exclusive**: if you pick one, you cannot pick the other.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Addition Principle.** If one task can be done in *m* ways and a second task in *n* ways, and the two tasks cannot both be done, then there are **m + n** ways to do the first task **or** the second.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example: the library choice.** The shelf has 5 mystery books and 8 science fiction books. You may borrow only one book.\n>\n> You pick a mystery book **or** a science fiction book: 5 + 8 = **13 choices**.\n\n## The Multiplication Principle (“and”)\n\nUse it when a task is done in **stages** — a sequence of choices that happen together.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Multiplication Principle.** If there are *m* ways to do one thing and *n* ways to do another after the first is done, there are **m × n** ways to do both.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example: password creator.** A 2-character code starts with a letter A, B or C and ends with a digit from 0 to 4.\n>\n> You pick a letter **and** a digit: 3 × 5 = **15 codes**: A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, B0, B1, B2, B3, B4, C0, C1, C2, C3, C4.\n\nAdding (3 + 5 = 8) would be wrong: every letter pairs with **every** digit, so each of the 3 letters gives 5 codes.\n\n| Principle | Key word | Logic |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Addition | **or** | You are choosing one from the groups. |\n| Multiplication | **and** | You are building a combination or a sequence. |\n\n## Using both principles together\n\nMany real problems have several **options**, and each option has several **steps**. Multiply inside each option, then add the options.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example: the prize package.** A contest winner chooses one sponsor’s package.\n> - Sponsor A: 3 types of tablets and 2 types of headphones (one of each).\n> - Sponsor B: 4 types of jerseys and 3 types of gym bags (one of each).\n>\n> Sponsor A: 3 × 2 = 6. Sponsor B: 4 × 3 = 12. Either package: 6 + 12 = **18 combinations**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example: City A to City C.**\n> - Option 1: one of 3 bus routes from A to B, then one of 2 train routes from B to C → 3 × 2 = 6 routes.\n> - Option 2: one of 4 direct flights → 4 routes.\n>\n> Total: 6 + 4 = **10 ways**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Adding every item (3 + 2 + 4 + 3) ignores the pairing inside each package. Always multiply the stages of one option **before** adding the options.\n\n## Watch\n\n![The Addition and Multiplication Principles](https://youtu.be/yTsbGglDo04)\n\n*Video: “01 The Addition and Multiplication Principles” by LMKMaths, on YouTube.*\n\n![The Fundamental Counting Principle](https://youtu.be/3lmEqp8VhAU)\n\n*Video: “The Fundamental Counting Principle” by The Organic Chemistry Tutor, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- A school offers 6 Arts and Design electives and 4 Sports electives; a student enrolls in one. How many choices? *(add)*\n- An ID code is one letter (A, B, C or D) followed by one digit (0–9). How many codes? *(multiply)*\n- A snack meal is one sandwich (Ham, Egg, Cheese, Chicken, Tuna) and one drink (Juice, Water, Milk). How many meals?\n- Bracket 1 pairs one of 5 Manila teams with one of 4 Quezon City teams; Bracket 2 pairs one of 6 Cavite teams with one of 2 Laguna teams. How many match-ups in all? *(both principles)*",
