        {
          "id": "f3535075-59e4-5ca4-99b8-8cfb053499f7",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 1.2: Permutations of Distinct Objects",
          "summary": "Ordered arrangements, factorials and the formula P(n, k) = n! ÷ (n − k)!.",
          "body": "## When order matters\n\nA **permutation** is an **ordered** arrangement of distinct objects. If changing the order gives a different outcome — a password, race results, class officers — you are counting permutations.\n\n- Choosing a President and a Vice President from 4 students: Ana–Ben is different from Ben–Ana. **Permutation.**\n- Forming a two-digit number from 7, 8 and 9 without repetition: 78 is not 87. **Permutation.**\n\n## Factorials\n\n**n!** (“n factorial”) is the product of the whole numbers from n down to 1: 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120. By definition, **0! = 1**.\n\n## Building the formula\n\nFill *k* positions from *n* distinct objects. The first position has n choices, the second n − 1, the third n − 2, and so on — the Multiplication Principle again:\n\n**P(n, k) = n × (n − 1) × … × (n − k + 1) = n! ÷ (n − k)!**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** P(6, 2) = 6! ÷ 4! = (6 × 5 × 4!) ÷ 4! = 6 × 5 = **30**.\n>\n> **Example 2.** P(7, 3) = 7! ÷ 4! = 7 × 6 × 5 = **210**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3: check by listing.** Arrange 2 of the letters A, B, C.\n>\n> Listing: AB, AC, BA, BC, CA, CB → 6 arrangements.\n> Formula: P(3, 2) = 3! ÷ 1! = 6. ✔\n\n## Permutations in real life\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Class officers.** From 10 students, how many ways can we elect a President, a Vice President and a Secretary?\n>\n> The positions are different, so order matters: P(10, 3) = 10! ÷ 7! = 10 × 9 × 8 = **720 ways**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **PIN codes.** How many 4-digit PINs use the digits 0–9 with no digit repeated?\n>\n> “1234” is not “4321”, so order matters: P(10, 4) = 10! ÷ 6! = 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = **5,040 PINs**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Two quick facts: **P(n, n) = n!** (arrange everything) and **P(n, 1) = n** (pick one). P(n, k) only makes sense when k ≤ n.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Choosing 3 of 10 students for a clean-up committee is **not** a permutation — the members have no different roles, so order doesn’t matter. That is a combination (next lesson).\n\n## Watch\n\n![Permutations and Combinations Tutorial](https://youtu.be/XJnIdRXUi7A)\n\n*Video: “Permutations and Combinations Tutorial” by The Organic Chemistry Tutor, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Permutation or not: assigning 3 of 10 students as President, Secretary and Treasurer?\n- How many 4-letter passwords can be made from A, B, C, D and E with no repeated letters?\n- List all two-letter codes from W, X, Y and Z without repetition, then check your count with P(4, 2).",
