          "title": "Lesson 1.4: Repeated Elements and Circular Arrangements",
          "summary": "Arrange words with repeated letters, seat people around a table, and string necklaces.",
          "body": "## Arrangements with repeated objects\n\nIf all n objects were different there would be n! arrangements. But in **MISSISSIPPI** the four S’s look the same: swapping two of them gives no new word. The plain n! **overcounts**.\n\nEach group of identical objects can be shuffled among themselves in (its size)! ways that all look the same, so divide by those factorials:\n\n**Number of distinct arrangements = n! ÷ (n₁! × n₂! × … × nₖ!)**\n\nwhere n is the total number of objects and n₁, n₂, …, nₖ are the counts of each repeated object.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1: MISSISSIPPI.** 11 letters: M (1), I (4), S (4), P (2).\n>\n> 11! ÷ (1! × 4! × 4! × 2!) = 39,916,800 ÷ 1,152 = **34,650**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2: BALLOON.** 7 letters with L (2) and O (2).\n>\n> 7! ÷ (2! × 2!) = 5,040 ÷ 4 = **1,260**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3: beads in a line.** 8 beads: 3 red, 3 blue, 2 green.\n>\n> 8! ÷ (3! × 3! × 2!) = 40,320 ÷ 72 = **560**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Three steps every time: count as if all are different (n!), find the repeats, then divide. Worked model: MAMA → 4! ÷ (2! × 2!) = 24 ÷ 4 = 6.\n\n## Circular arrangements\n\nAround a round table there is no “first seat”. Rotating everyone one seat keeps every person’s neighbours the same, so A-B-C-D is the same arrangement as B-C-D-A. Each circular arrangement matches **n** linear ones (one per starting point):\n\n**Circular arrangements of n distinct objects = n! ÷ n = (n − 1)!**\n\nAnother way to see it: fix one person’s seat, then arrange the other n − 1 people freely.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** 8 people around a round table: (8 − 1)! = 7! = **5,040**.\n>\n> **Example 2.** 7 different flowers in a circular wreath: 6! = **720**.\n>\n> **Example 3.** 8 guests on a merry-go-round (it turns but can’t be flipped), rotations the same: 7! = **5,040**.\n\n## Necklaces and key rings: flipping counts too\n\nA necklace or key ring can be **turned over**, so an arrangement and its mirror image are the same. Divide by 2 as well:\n\n**Arrangements when rotations and reflections are the same = (n − 1)! ÷ 2**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Necklace.** 6 different beads: 5! ÷ 2 = 120 ÷ 2 = **60**.\n>\n> **Key ring.** 5 distinct keys: 4! ÷ 2 = 24 ÷ 2 = **12**.\n\n| Situation | Formula |\n| --- | --- |\n| n distinct objects in a row | n! |\n| Row with repeated objects | n! ÷ (n₁! × n₂! × …) |\n| n distinct objects in a circle | (n − 1)! |\n| Circle that can be flipped (necklace, key ring) | (n − 1)! ÷ 2 |\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Books on a shelf, students in a line and letters on a banner are **linear**: use n!. Only seats around a round table, a wreath or a ring are circular.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Permutations with repeated letters](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oO1ElSEyFsQ)\n\n*Video: a walkthrough of permutations with repeated letters, on YouTube.*\n\n![Circular permutations](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vO00VK-eTL8)\n\n*Video: a walkthrough of circular permutations, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- How many distinct arrangements of the letters of MUSICALLY? Of CONCERT?\n- Nine VIP guests sit around a circular table. How many seatings?\n- Six different charms are strung on a bracelet that can be turned over. How many bracelets?",
          "objectives": [
            "Count the permutations of a set with non-distinct (repeated) elements",
            "Count the arrangements of distinct objects in a circle",
            "Differentiate linear and circular arrangements and apply the right formula"
          ],
