        {
          "id": "3772a026-8121-53e3-923b-fc5dc8cf2227",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 1.5: Combinatorial Counting Techniques and Their Applications",
          "summary": "Distribute distinct and identical objects, use stars and bars, and solve multi-step problems.",
          "body": "## A. Distinct objects into distinct groups\n\nEach of *n* different objects goes into one of *r* labelled groups. Every object has r choices, independently — the Multiplication Principle *n* times:\n\n**Number of distributions = rⁿ** (n distinct objects, r distinct groups, groups may be empty)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** 4 distinct books among 3 students: 3⁴ = **81**.\n>\n> **Example 2.** 6 distinct tasks among 4 workers: 4⁶ = **4,096**.\n>\n> **Example 3.** Anna, Ben, Carla, David and Ella each join the Math, Science or Arts Club: 3⁵ = **243**.\n\n### With restrictions: subtract what isn’t allowed\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 4.** 3 students into 2 groups, Group 1 must have at least one student. Total 2³ = 8; the only bad case is everyone in Group 2. 8 − 1 = **7**.\n>\n> **Example 5.** 4 distinct balls into 2 boxes, no box empty. Total 2⁴ = 16; bad cases: all in Box A, all in Box B. 16 − 2 = **14**.\n\n### Fixed group sizes\n\nIf the sizes are fixed, fill the groups one at a time with combinations. 6 different kits into 3 committees of 2 each: C(6, 2) × C(4, 2) × C(2, 2) = 15 × 6 × 1 = **90** (the same as 6! ÷ (2! × 2! × 2!)).\n\n## B. Identical objects into distinct groups: stars and bars\n\nWhen the objects are identical, only **how many** each group gets matters. Picture the n objects as stars and use r − 1 bars to split them into r groups. For 5 candies among 3 children, ★★|★|★★ means 2, 1, 2. Every arrangement of n stars and r − 1 bars is one distribution:\n\n**Number of distributions = C(n + r − 1, r − 1)** (n identical objects, r distinct groups, groups may be empty)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** 10 identical candies among 4 children: C(13, 3) = **286**.\n>\n> **Example 2.** 12 identical books on 5 shelves, empty shelves allowed: C(16, 4) = **1,820**.\n\n**Each group must get at least one?** Give every group one object first, then share the rest. This gives **C(n − 1, r − 1)**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3.** 7 identical coins into 3 boxes, none empty. Reserve 3 coins (one per box), share the other 4: C(4 + 3 − 1, 2) = C(6, 2) = **15**.\n\n## C. Choosing the correct technique\n\n| Situation | Key feature | Technique |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Objects are distinct | order / assignment matters | multiplication principle, permutations, rⁿ |\n| Objects are identical | only the counts matter | stars and bars |\n| Arrangements with conditions | restrictions exist | casework or complement (subtract) |\n| Selection only | no arrangement | combinations |\n\n- Assign 4 different tasks to 3 students → distinct objects into distinct groups (3⁴).\n- Distribute 6 identical balls into 4 boxes → stars and bars, C(9, 3).\n- Choose 3 leaders from 10 students → combinations, C(10, 3).\n\n## Multi-step problems\n\nBreak the problem into stages, count each stage with the right technique, then multiply the stages.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** From 12 applicants, 5 leaders are chosen for 5 different positions, then 10 identical supply packs are shared among them.\n>\n> Positions: P(12, 5) = 95,040. Supplies: C(10 + 5 − 1, 4) = C(14, 4) = 1,001.\n> Total: 95,040 × 1,001 = **95,135,040** outcomes.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Read for the key words first: **arrange** (order), **choose** (no order), **distribute**, **identical**, **distinct**.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Distributing identical objects: stars and bars](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iRnBryQ8RjU)\n\n*Video: a walkthrough of distributing identical objects into groups, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Ten identical snack packs go to sections 11-A, 11-B and 11-C. How many distributions?\n- Twelve identical food packs go to 4 evacuation centers. Show the stars-and-bars setup.\n- Seven different school supplies go to 3 barangay centers, each receiving at least 2. Why must the sizes be 3, 2, 2?",
          "objectives": [
            "Count the ways to distribute distinct objects into distinct groups",
            "Count the ways to distribute identical objects into distinct groups using stars and bars",
            "Choose the right counting technique for a problem",
            "Solve multi-step counting problems that combine several techniques"
