    {
      "id": "88d1205f-c856-5645-8854-ddcd81b44b4f",
      "title": "Unit 2: Probability",
      "description": "Sample spaces and events, the addition rule, independence, conditional probability and Bayes’ Rule — measuring chance to make better decisions.",
      "activities": [
        {
          "id": "a49f6a53-b130-5fc0-ab77-1ead7d8231b7",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 2.1: Probability of an event",
          "summary": "Random experiments, sample spaces and events, and P(E) = n(E) ÷ n(S) by listing and by counting.",
          "body": "## Random experiments, outcomes and sample spaces\n\nA **random experiment** is a process whose result cannot be predicted with certainty — tossing a coin, rolling a die, or drawing a name from a box of folded papers. Each possible result is an **outcome**.\n\n- The **sample space** S is the set of *all* possible outcomes. We write n(S) for the number of outcomes in it.\n- An **event** E is a subset of the sample space: the outcomes we are interested in. n(E) is the number of outcomes in E.\n\n| Random experiment | Sample space S | n(S) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Toss a fair coin once | {H, T} | 2 |\n| Roll a fair die | {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} | 6 |\n| Toss a fair coin twice | {HH, HT, TH, TT} | 4 |\n| Pick a letter of MATH from a closed box | {M, A, T, H} | 4 |\n| Toss a coin and roll a die | {H1, H2, …, H6, T1, T2, …, T6} | 12 |\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Tossing two coins has **four** outcomes, not three. HT (head first) and TH (tail first) are different outcomes. Listing them in order keeps you from missing one.\n\n## The probability of an event\n\nWhen every outcome is **equally likely**, the probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the number of possible outcomes:\n\n**P(E) = n(E) ÷ n(S)**\n\n- Probabilities are always between 0 and 1: **0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1**.\n- P(E) = 0 means E cannot happen (it has no favorable outcomes).\n- P(E) = 1 means E is certain (every outcome is favorable).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — Rolling a fair die.** S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, so n(S) = 6.\n>\n> Event “an even number”: E = {2, 4, 6}, so n(E) = 3.\n>\n> P(even) = 3/6 = **1/2**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — A letter from MATH.** S = {M, A, T, H} and the only vowel is A.\n>\n> P(vowel) = **1/4**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — Two coins.** S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}. Event “exactly one head” = {HT, TH}.\n>\n> P(exactly one head) = 2/4 = **1/2**.\n\n## Using counting techniques\n\nListing works for small experiments. When the sample space is large, count it instead with the multiplication principle, permutations or combinations from Unit 1.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 4 — A coin and a die.** By the multiplication principle, n(S) = 2 × 6 = 12.\n>\n> Event “a head and an even number”: H2, H4, H6, so n(E) = 3.\n>\n> P = 3/12 = **1/4**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 5 — Choosing a committee.** A committee of 2 is chosen at random from 6 learners, 4 of whom are girls. What is the probability that both members are girls?\n>\n> n(S) = C(6, 2) = 15 possible committees.\n>\n> n(E) = C(4, 2) = 6 committees of two girls.\n>\n> P(both girls) = 6/15 = **2/5**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> A standard deck has 52 cards: 4 suits (hearts, diamonds, clubs, spades) of 13 ranks each. The **face cards** are the Jack, Queen and King of each suit — 12 in all.\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Roll a fair die. What is P(rolling a multiple of 3)? *(2/6 = 1/3)*\n- Roll a fair die. What is P(a number greater than 4)? *(2/6 = 1/3)*\n- Roll two fair dice. What is P(both numbers are the same)? *(6/36 = 1/6)*\n- Why can a probability never be 1.2?",
          "objectives": [
            "List the sample space and the events of a random experiment",
            "Find the probability of an event by listing outcomes",
            "Find the probability of an event using counting techniques"
          ],
          "difficulty": 1,
          "estimatedMinutes": 15,
          "maxAttempts": null,
          "passingScore": 0,
          "feedbackMode": "immediate",
          "allowSkills": false,
          "boss": null,
          "prerequisiteIds": [],
          "questions": []
        },
