        {
          "id": "985f3783-da33-5d6d-bd8b-3f82c23865bf",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 2.2: Addition of probabilities",
          "summary": "Mutually exclusive events, the addition rule for “A or B”, and the complement rule.",
          "body": "## Mutually exclusive events\n\nTwo events are **mutually exclusive** when they cannot happen at the same time — they have **no outcomes in common**, so A ∩ B is empty.\n\n- Rolling a 3 and rolling a 6 on one roll of a die: mutually exclusive.\n- Drawing a heart and drawing a king from a deck: **not** mutually exclusive, because the king of hearts belongs to both events.\n\n## The addition rule\n\nThe addition rule gives the probability that **at least one** of two events happens (A ∪ B, read “A or B”).\n\n**If A and B are mutually exclusive:** P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B)\n\n**If A and B are not mutually exclusive:** P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)\n\nThe second rule always works: for mutually exclusive events P(A ∩ B) = 0, so it becomes the first.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — Rolling a die.** A = an even number = {2, 4, 6}; B = a number greater than 4 = {5, 6}. The overlap is {6}.\n>\n> P(A ∪ B) = 3/6 + 2/6 − 1/6 = 4/6 = **2/3**.\n>\n> Check by listing: A ∪ B = {2, 4, 5, 6}, and 4/6 = 2/3.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — A heart or a king.** P(heart) = 13/52, P(king) = 4/52, P(king of hearts) = 1/52.\n>\n> P(heart ∪ king) = 13/52 + 4/52 − 1/52 = 16/52 = **4/13**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Adding without subtracting the overlap counts the shared outcomes **twice**. Before adding, always ask: “Can both happen at once?”\n\n## The complement of an event\n\nThe **complement** of A, written A′, is every outcome that is **not** in A. Since A and A′ together make up the whole sample space and never overlap:\n\n**P(A′) = 1 − P(A)**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — At least one head.** Toss a coin 3 times. The only outcome with no head is TTT, so P(no head) = 1/8.\n>\n> P(at least one head) = 1 − 1/8 = **7/8** — much faster than listing the 7 favorable outcomes.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> When a question says “at least one”, try the complement first.\n\n## Reading a survey with overlap\n\nA class of 40 students was surveyed: 18 like Mathematics, 15 like English, and 7 like both.\n\n| Group | Students |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mathematics only | 18 − 7 = 11 |\n| Both subjects | 7 |\n| English only | 15 − 7 = 8 |\n| Neither | 40 − 26 = 14 |\n| Total | 40 |\n\nP(likes Mathematics or English) = 18/40 + 15/40 − 7/40 = 26/40 = **13/20**. Adding 18 + 15 alone would count the 7 students who like both twice.\n\n## Check yourself\n\n| Situation | Mutually exclusive? | P(A or B) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Die: 3 or 6 | Yes | 2/6 = 1/3 |\n| Die: even or less than 4 | No (2 is in both) | 3/6 + 3/6 − 1/6 = 5/6 |\n| Card: heart or spade | Yes | 13/52 + 13/52 = 1/2 |\n| Card: heart or face card | No (3 heart face cards) | 13/52 + 12/52 − 3/52 = 11/26 |\n| Ball numbered 1 to 6: prime or even | No (2 is in both) | 3/6 + 3/6 − 1/6 = 5/6 |",
          "objectives": [
            "Decide whether two or more events are mutually exclusive",
            "Find the probability of the complement of an event",
            "Find the probability of the union of two events"
          ],
          "difficulty": 2,
          "estimatedMinutes": 15,
          "maxAttempts": null,
          "passingScore": 0,
          "feedbackMode": "immediate",
          "allowSkills": false,
          "boss": null,
          "prerequisiteIds": [
            "e6649a46-073d-524e-bbc0-c6bff0dfadc7"
          ],
          "questions": []
        },
