          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 2.3: Independent events",
          "summary": "When one event doesn’t affect another, with and without replacement, and how to test for independence.",
          "body": "## When one event doesn’t affect another\n\nTwo events A and B are **independent** if the occurrence of one does **not** change the probability of the other. Independence usually comes from experiments that are separate, repeated, or done **with replacement**.\n\n**A and B are independent exactly when P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B).**\n\nIf the events are **dependent**, the second probability must be adjusted for what already happened:\n\n**P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B | A)**, where P(B | A) is the probability of B once A has happened.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — A coin and a die.** A fair coin is tossed and a fair die is rolled. A = heads, B = rolling a 4. The coin can’t influence the die, so the events are independent.\n>\n> P(A ∩ B) = 1/2 × 1/6 = **1/12**.\n\n## With and without replacement\n\nA box holds 3 red and 2 blue balls. Two balls are drawn. What is the chance both are red?\n\n| How the balls are drawn | 1st draw red | 2nd draw red | Both red |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| With replacement (independent) | 3/5 | 3/5 | 9/25 |\n| Without replacement (dependent) | 3/5 | 2/4 | 3/10 |\n\nWith replacement, the box is the same for the second draw, so its chance holds. Without replacement, one red ball is gone: 2 red balls remain among 4, so the second chance changes.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — Two hearts without replacement.** Two cards are drawn from a deck without replacement.\n>\n> P(heart, then heart) = 13/52 × 12/51 = 156/2652 = **1/17**.\n>\n> With replacement it would be 13/52 × 13/52 = **1/16**.\n\n## Testing for independence\n\nTo check two events, compute P(A ∩ B) directly and compare it with P(A) · P(B).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — A king and a heart.** One card is drawn. A = a king, B = a heart.\n>\n> P(A ∩ B) = P(king of hearts) = 1/52.\n>\n> P(A) · P(B) = 4/52 × 13/52 = 1/13 × 1/4 = 1/52.\n>\n> They are equal, so “king” and “heart” are **independent**: knowing the card is a heart doesn’t change the chance it is a king.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Independent is **not** the same as mutually exclusive. Mutually exclusive events can’t happen together (P(A ∩ B) = 0). Independent events can happen together — one just doesn’t affect the other.\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Coin tossed twice: P(head on both)? *(1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4)*\n- Die rolled twice: P(a 2, then a 5)? *(1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36)*\n- Marbles with replacement (5 red, 3 blue): P(red, then red)? *(5/8 × 5/8 = 25/64)*\n- Why does drawing without replacement make the events dependent?",
          "objectives": [
            "Decide whether two events are independent",
            "Find the probability that two events both occur"
          ],
          "difficulty": 2,
          "estimatedMinutes": 15,
          "maxAttempts": null,
