        {
          "id": "d2ac6fd7-ce2b-5d5f-87c5-78c5dc74bcd3",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 2.4: Conditional probability",
          "summary": "Probability given a condition: the shrinking sample space, two-way tables and draws without replacement.",
          "body": "## Probability with extra information\n\nA **simple probability** looks at the whole sample space. A **conditional probability** is the probability that event B happens **given** that event A has already happened. It is written P(B | A) and read “the probability of B given A”.\n\n**P(B | A) = P(A ∩ B) ÷ P(A)**, provided that P(A) > 0\n\nOnce the condition is known, the sample space **shrinks** to the outcomes in A. With equally likely outcomes this is simply:\n\n**P(B | A) = n(A ∩ B) ÷ n(A)**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — A card given a condition.** A card is drawn from a well-shuffled deck. A = the card is a face card; B = the card is a king.\n>\n> Given that the card is a face card, only the 12 face cards remain possible, and 4 of them are kings.\n>\n> P(king | face card) = 4/12 = **1/3**, while the simple probability P(king) = 4/52 = 1/13.\n\n## Reading a two-way table\n\nA survey of 200 students recorded club membership and sports participation.\n\n| Student | Sports | No sport | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| In a club | 50 | 30 | 80 |\n| Not in a club | 70 | 50 | 120 |\n| Total | 120 | 80 | 200 |\n\n- **Simple:** P(plays sports) = 120/200 = **3/5**.\n- **Given a club member:** the denominator is the 80 club members. P(sports | club) = 50/80 = **5/8**.\n- **Given a sports player:** the denominator is the 120 who play sports. P(club | sports) = 50/120 = **5/12**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> P(B | A) and P(A | B) are usually **different**. The numerator (50) is the same, but the condition decides the denominator. Always circle the group you are told about first.\n\n## Drawing without replacement\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2.** A box contains 5 red and 3 blue balls. Two are drawn one after the other without replacement. Given that the first ball is red, what is the probability the second is blue?\n>\n> After a red ball is removed, 7 balls remain: 4 red and 3 blue.\n>\n> P(2nd blue | 1st red) = **3/7**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3.** A feeding-program tray has 9 pandesal and 6 rice cakes. Two items are taken without replacement. Given that the first is pandesal, 8 pandesal remain among 14 items.\n>\n> P(2nd pandesal | 1st pandesal) = 8/14 = **4/7**.\n\n## Conditional probability and independence\n\nIf A and B are independent, the condition changes nothing: **P(A | B) = P(A)**. Comparing a conditional probability with the simple one is a quick test for independence.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Four steps: (1) name the condition — the group you’re told about; (2) count that group: the denominator; (3) inside that group, count those that fit the event: the numerator; (4) simplify.\n\n## Check yourself\n\nA barangay surveyed 150 residents about exercise and blood pressure.\n\n| Resident | Normal | High | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Exercises | 40 | 20 | 60 |\n| Does not exercise | 30 | 60 | 90 |\n| Total | 70 | 80 | 150 |\n\n- P(normal blood pressure)? *(70/150 = 7/15 ≈ 0.47)*\n- P(normal | exercises)? *(40/60 = 2/3 ≈ 0.67)*\n- What does comparing the two answers suggest about exercise and blood pressure for these residents?",
          "objectives": [
            "Tell simple probability apart from conditional probability",
            "Calculate conditional probabilities from tables, formulas and draws"
          ],
          "difficulty": 3,
          "estimatedMinutes": 20,
          "maxAttempts": null,
          "passingScore": 0,
          "feedbackMode": "immediate",
          "allowSkills": false,
          "boss": null,
          "prerequisiteIds": [
            "c676356b-a5bd-510d-b453-68f21329aed6"
          ],
          "questions": []
        },
