        {
          "id": "d3715b82-96ff-58cf-9eaa-22bcb79ac84b",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Lesson 2.5: Bayes’ Rule",
          "summary": "Updating a probability with new evidence: priors, likelihoods, total probability and posteriors.",
          "body": "## Reasoning backwards from a result\n\nOften we know how likely a **result** is for each possible **cause** — how often each factory makes a defective phone, or how often a test is positive for sick and healthy people. Bayes’ Rule turns this around: given the result, how likely is each cause?\n\n**P(A | B) = P(B | A) · P(A) ÷ P(B)**, where P(B) > 0\n\nThe denominator comes from the **law of total probability**, adding up every way B can happen:\n\n**P(B) = P(B | A) · P(A) + P(B | A′) · P(A′)**\n\n- **Prior** P(A): how likely the cause is before any evidence.\n- **Likelihood** P(B | A): how likely the result is if the cause is true.\n- **Posterior** P(A | B): the updated probability of the cause once the result is known.\n\n## Worked example: two factories\n\nFactory 1 and Factory 2 each supply 50% of a store’s phones. The defect rate is 2% for Factory 1 and 4% for Factory 2. A phone is found to be defective. What is P(Factory 2 | defective)?\n\n| Factory | Prior | Defect rate | Prior × defect rate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Factory 1 | 0.50 | 0.02 | 0.010 |\n| Factory 2 | 0.50 | 0.04 | 0.020 |\n| Total = P(defective) | | | 0.030 |\n\nP(Factory 2 | defective) = 0.020 ÷ 0.030 = **2/3 ≈ 0.67**. Factory 2 makes defects twice as often, so a defective phone is twice as likely to come from it.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Read each row of the table as one branch of a tree diagram: the first branch is the cause (its prior), the second is the result (its likelihood). Multiply along each branch, add the branches that end in the result, then divide.\n\n## Worked example: a medical test\n\nA rare illness affects 1% of a population. A test detects it 99% of the time, but it is also positive for 5% of healthy people. A person tests positive. What is P(illness | positive)?\n\nImagine 10,000 people:\n\n| Group | People | Test positive |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Have the illness (1%) | 100 | 99 (99% of 100) |\n| Healthy (99%) | 9,900 | 495 (5% of 9,900) |\n| Total | 10,000 | 594 |\n\nP(illness | positive) = 99 ÷ 594 = **1/6 ≈ 0.17**. With the formula: (0.99 × 0.01) ÷ (0.99 × 0.01 + 0.05 × 0.99) = 0.0099 ÷ 0.0594 ≈ 0.17.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A test that is “99% accurate” can still give mostly false alarms when the illness is rare: the 5% of the many healthy people outnumber the 99% of the few sick people. Always weigh the **prior** (how common the cause is) together with the test’s accuracy.\n\n## More than two causes\n\nThe rule extends to any number of causes: the denominator adds one product for each cause, and the posteriors of all the causes add up to 1.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Machine 1 makes 80% of the bottles with a 1% defect rate; Machine 2 makes 20% with a 5% defect rate.\n>\n> P(defective) = 0.80 × 0.01 + 0.20 × 0.05 = 0.008 + 0.010 = 0.018.\n>\n> P(Machine 2 | defective) = 0.010 ÷ 0.018 = **5/9 ≈ 0.56** — even though Machine 2 makes only 20% of the bottles.\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Suppliers A and B each provide 50% of a part; the defect rate is 3% for A and 5% for B. Find P(B | defective). *(0.025 ÷ 0.040 = 5/8)*\n- Across the examples, what made a posterior large or small — the prior or the likelihood?\n- Why must the posteriors of all the causes add up to 1?",
          "objectives": [
            "Explain Bayes’ Rule and how it reverses a conditional probability",
            "Apply Bayes’ Rule to find the probability of a cause given a result"
          ],
          "difficulty": 4,
          "estimatedMinutes": 25,
          "maxAttempts": null,
          "passingScore": 0,
          "feedbackMode": "immediate",
          "allowSkills": false,
          "boss": null,
          "prerequisiteIds": [
            "0419dee2-8c48-5dad-b3aa-4af7e12d407f"
          ],
          "questions": []
        },
