    {
      "id": "da481c92-d349-52cd-b16d-2df330003e4b",
      "title": "Unit 3: Number Theory",
      "description": "Divisibility, primes, GCD and LCM, Diophantine equations and modular arithmetic — ending with the check digits on barcodes, books and cards.",
      "activities": [
        {
          "id": "f033d34a-370a-5c17-b0e6-ab5df4531208",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Divisibility and its properties",
          "summary": "What a ∣ b means, the basic properties, and how to prove or disprove them.",
          "body": "## What does “divides” mean?\n\nFor integers a and b with a ≠ 0, we say **a divides b**, written **a ∣ b**, when there is an integer k such that\n\n**b = a · k**\n\nThen a is a **divisor** (or factor) of b, and b is a **multiple** of a. When no such integer k exists we write **a ∤ b**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** 6 ∣ 18 because 18 = 6 · 3, and 3 is an integer.\n>\n> 6 ∤ 20 because 20 ÷ 6 = 3.33…, which is not an integer.\n>\n> The multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24, … — picture them as equally spaced jumps on a number line.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Read a ∣ b as “a divides b”, not “a divided by b”. 3 ∣ 12 is a true **statement**; 12 ÷ 3 = 4 is a **number**. And the order matters: 3 ∣ 12 is true, but 12 ∣ 3 is false.\n\n## Basic properties of divisibility\n\nFor integers a, b and c:\n\n1. **Sum and difference:** if a ∣ b and a ∣ c, then a ∣ (b + c) and a ∣ (b − c).\n2. **Transitive:** if a ∣ b and b ∣ c, then a ∣ c.\n3. **Multiple:** if a ∣ b, then a ∣ (b · k) for any integer k.\n4. **Linear combination:** if a ∣ b and a ∣ c, then a ∣ (mb + nc) for any integers m and n.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — checking with numbers.** Let a = 3, b = 9, c = 12.\n>\n> 3 ∣ 9 and 3 ∣ 12.\n>\n> b + c = 21, and 21 ÷ 3 = 7, so 3 ∣ (b + c).\n>\n> b − c = −3, and −3 ÷ 3 = −1, so 3 ∣ (b − c).\n\n## Proving a property from the definition\n\nEvery proof in this lesson follows the same three moves: **write** each divisibility as a product, **combine** the expressions, and **factor out** a to show the result is a times an integer.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — proof of the sum property.** Suppose a ∣ b and a ∣ c.\n>\n> By the definition, b = a · k₁ and c = a · k₂ for some integers k₁ and k₂.\n>\n> Then b + c = a · k₁ + a · k₂ = a(k₁ + k₂).\n>\n> Since k₁ + k₂ is an integer, a ∣ (b + c). ∎\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Proof of the transitive property.** Suppose a ∣ b and b ∣ c, so b = a · k₁ and c = b · k₂.\n>\n> Substitute: c = (a · k₁) · k₂ = a(k₁k₂). Since k₁k₂ is an integer, a ∣ c. ∎\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Proof of the linear-combination property.** If b = a · k₁ and c = a · k₂, then\n>\n> mb + nc = m(a · k₁) + n(a · k₂) = a(mk₁ + nk₂).\n>\n> mk₁ + nk₂ is an integer, so a ∣ (mb + nc). For example, with m = 2 and n = 3, a ∣ (2b + 3c). ∎\n\n## Disproving a claim: counterexamples\n\nOne example where a statement fails is enough to show it is false.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Claim:** if a ∣ (b + c), then a ∣ b and a ∣ c.\n>\n> **Counterexample:** 5 ∣ (2 + 3), because 5 ∣ 5 — but 5 ∤ 2 and 5 ∤ 3. So the converse of the sum property is **false**.\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Is 7 ∣ 0 true? (Yes: 0 = 7 · 0.)\n- If 4 ∣ n, must 4 ∣ 10n? (Yes — the multiple property.)\n- If 4 ∣ 8, must 4 ∣ (8 + 10)? (No: 18 = 4 · 4.5, and 4.5 is not an integer.)",
          "objectives": [
            "Define divisibility: a ∣ b when b = a · k for some integer k",
            "Use the sum, difference, multiple and transitive properties of divisibility",
            "Justify divisibility properties directly from the definition"
          ],
