        {
          "id": "32c7c984-00d7-5a27-afa0-585e980a7e19",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "GCD and LCM: prime factorization and the Euclidean algorithm",
          "summary": "Two ways to find the GCD, and the LCM from a · b = gcd · lcm.",
          "body": "## Two key ideas\n\n- The **greatest common divisor (GCD)** of two integers is the largest positive integer that divides both exactly. It answers “equal grouping” and “fair sharing” questions.\n- The **least common multiple (LCM)** is the smallest positive integer that is a multiple of both. It answers “when will these repeating events line up again?” questions.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **By listing (small numbers only).** Divisors of 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, **6**, 12. Divisors of 18: 1, 2, 3, **6**, 9, 18. Common divisors: 1, 2, 3, 6, so gcd(12, 18) = 6.\n>\n> Multiples of 12: 12, 24, **36**, 48, … Multiples of 18: 18, **36**, 54, … So lcm(12, 18) = 36.\n\n## Method 1: prime factorization\n\n- **GCD:** take the primes the two numbers share, each with its **lowest** exponent.\n- **LCM:** take **every** prime that appears, each with its **highest** exponent.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** Find the GCD and LCM of 24 and 36.\n>\n> 24 = 2³ × 3 and 36 = 2² × 3²\n>\n> GCD (lowest exponents): 2² × 3 = **12**\n>\n> LCM (highest exponents): 2³ × 3² = **72**\n\n## Method 2: the Euclidean algorithm\n\nDivide the larger number by the smaller, then divide the previous divisor by the remainder. Repeat until the remainder is 0. The **last nonzero remainder** is the GCD.\n\nIt works because of the rule **gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b)**: any number that divides both a and b also divides a − qb (the remainder), and the other way round, so the pair keeps the same common divisors while the numbers shrink.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2.** Find gcd(48, 18).\n>\n> 48 = 18(2) + 12\n>\n> 18 = 12(1) + 6\n>\n> 12 = 6(2) + 0\n>\n> The last nonzero remainder is 6, so **gcd(48, 18) = 6**.\n\n## The GCD–LCM relationship\n\nFor any two positive integers a and b:\n\n**a · b = gcd(a, b) · lcm(a, b)**\n\nSo once you have the GCD (by the fast Euclidean algorithm), the LCM is lcm(a, b) = (a · b) ÷ gcd(a, b).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3.** Find lcm(12, 18), given gcd(12, 18) = 6.\n>\n> 12 · 18 = 6 · lcm(12, 18)\n>\n> 216 = 6 · lcm(12, 18)\n>\n> **lcm(12, 18) = 36**\n\n## Which method is faster?\n\nPrime factorization is quick for small, familiar numbers. For large numbers, finding the prime factors can be very slow, while the Euclidean algorithm only needs a few divisions.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 4.** gcd(204, 85):\n>\n> 204 = 85(2) + 34\n>\n> 85 = 34(2) + 17\n>\n> 34 = 17(2) + 0\n>\n> **gcd = 17**, in three divisions — no need to discover that 204 = 2² × 3 × 17 and 85 = 5 × 17.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Real life.** GCD: 24 pandesal and 36 ensaymada packed into identical bags with nothing left over — at most gcd(24, 36) = 12 bags. LCM: one jeepney leaves every 12 minutes and another every 18 minutes — they leave together again after lcm(12, 18) = 36 minutes.",
          "objectives": [
            "Define the GCD and LCM of two positive integers",
            "Compute the GCD and LCM using prime factorization",
            "Compute the GCD with the Euclidean algorithm",
            "Find the LCM using a · b = gcd(a, b) · lcm(a, b)"
          ],
