        {
          "id": "4208cce2-5444-5410-ad4c-8c93136cb033",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Solving linear Diophantine equations",
          "summary": "When ax + by = c has integer solutions, and how to find all of them.",
          "body": "## What is a linear Diophantine equation?\n\nAn equation **ax + by = c**, where a, b and c are integers and we want **integer** solutions x and y. These model “exact total” problems: buying items at fixed prices with an exact budget, or making an amount from two coin values.\n\n## When do solutions exist?\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> ax + by = c has integer solutions **if and only if gcd(a, b) ∣ c**.\n\nWhy: gcd(a, b) divides both a and b, so it divides ax + by for every x and y. If it does not divide c, the two sides can never be equal.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1.** Does 12x + 18y = 6 have integer solutions?\n>\n> gcd(12, 18) = 6, and 6 ∣ 6, so **yes**.\n>\n> By contrast, 8x + 12y = 14 has **no** integer solutions: gcd(8, 12) = 4 and 4 ∤ 14.\n\n## The extended Euclidean algorithm\n\nRun the Euclidean algorithm, then work **backwards** through the divisions to write the GCD as a combination of a and b.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2.** Find gcd(20, 35).\n>\n> 35 = 20(1) + 15\n>\n> 20 = 15(1) + 5\n>\n> 15 = 5(3) + 0\n>\n> So **gcd(20, 35) = 5**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3.** Express gcd(20, 35) as a linear combination.\n>\n> From the second line: 5 = 20 − 15\n>\n> From the first line: 15 = 35 − 20\n>\n> Substitute: 5 = 20 − (35 − 20) = 2(20) − 35\n>\n> So **5 = 2(20) − 1(35)**.\n\n## From the GCD to a particular solution\n\nIf d = gcd(a, b) = a · m + b · n and d ∣ c, multiply both sides by **c ÷ d**:\n\nx₀ = m · (c ÷ d), y₀ = n · (c ÷ d)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 4.** Solve 20x + 35y = 15.\n>\n> gcd(20, 35) = 5 and 5 ∣ 15, so solutions exist. Multiply 5 = 2(20) − 1(35) by 15 ÷ 5 = 3:\n>\n> 15 = 6(20) − 3(35), so **x₀ = 6, y₀ = −3**.\n>\n> Check: 20(6) + 35(−3) = 120 − 105 = 15 ✓\n\n## The general solution\n\nOnce one solution (x₀, y₀) is known, **all** integer solutions are\n\n**x = x₀ + (b ÷ d)t, y = y₀ − (a ÷ d)t**, for any integer t\n\nAdding b ÷ d to x and subtracting a ÷ d from y keeps ax + by the same, so there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> For 20x + 35y = 15: d = 5, b ÷ d = 7, a ÷ d = 4.\n>\n> **x = 6 + 7t, y = −3 − 4t.** (t = −1 gives x = −1, y = 1: −20 + 35 = 15 ✓)\n\n## Real-life problems: only whole, non-negative answers\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 5.** A canteen sells sandwiches at ₱35 and juice at ₱25. A class spends exactly ₱700. How many of each could they have bought?\n>\n> 35x + 25y = 700. gcd(35, 25) = 5 and 5 ∣ 700, so solutions exist. Divide by 5: 7x + 5y = 140.\n>\n> y = (140 − 7x) ÷ 5 is a whole number when x is a multiple of 5:\n\n| Sandwiches x | Juice y | Check |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 28 | 0 + 700 = 700 |\n| 5 | 21 | 175 + 525 = 700 |\n| 10 | 14 | 350 + 350 = 700 |\n| 15 | 7 | 525 + 175 = 700 |\n| 20 | 0 | 700 + 0 = 700 |\n\nNegative counts make no sense here, so the infinitely many integer solutions shrink to just **5 practical plans**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Always check a solution by substituting it back into the original equation.",
          "objectives": [
            "Decide whether ax + by = c has integer solutions using gcd(a, b) ∣ c",
            "Express gcd(a, b) as a linear combination with the extended Euclidean algorithm",
            "Find a particular solution and the general solution of a linear Diophantine equation",
            "Solve real-life problems on exact totals with linear Diophantine equations"
          ],
          "difficulty": 3,
          "estimatedMinutes": 30,
          "maxAttempts": null,
