        {
          "id": "767dc612-76c3-5f7a-bc76-6eef45c67761",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Congruence modulo m and modular arithmetic",
          "summary": "Remainder families, clocks and calendars, properties, and fast modular powers.",
          "body": "## The modulo operation and congruence\n\n**a mod m** is the remainder when a is divided by m. For example, 23 mod 4 = 3, because 23 = 4 × 5 + 3.\n\nTwo integers are **congruent modulo m** when they leave the same remainder on division by m. Equivalently:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **a ≡ b (mod m)** if and only if **m ∣ (a − b)**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** 19 mod 5 = 4 and 9 mod 5 = 4, so **19 ≡ 9 (mod 5)** — read “19 is congruent to 9 modulo 5”.\n>\n> Check with the definition: 19 − 9 = 10, and 5 ∣ 10. ✓\n\nNote the difference: a mod m is a **single number** (a remainder); a ≡ b (mod m) is a **relationship** between two numbers.\n\n## Equivalence classes: remainder families\n\nAll integers with the same remainder modulo m form one **equivalence class**. Modulo 5, the class of 4 is {…, −1, 4, 9, 14, 19, …}: each differs from 4 by a multiple of 5 — 5 ∣ (9 − 4), 5 ∣ (14 − 4), 5 ∣ (19 − 4). Modulo 5 there are exactly five classes, for the remainders 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4.\n\n## Cyclic systems\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clock (mod 12).** It is 8:00 and 7 hours pass. 8 + 7 = 15 and 15 ≡ 3 (mod 12), so the clock shows **3:00**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Days of the week (mod 7).** Number the days Sunday = 0, Monday = 1, Tuesday = 2, Wednesday = 3, Thursday = 4, Friday = 5, Saturday = 6. Today is Tuesday (2) and 10 days pass: 2 + 10 = 12 and 12 ≡ 5 (mod 7), so it will be **Friday**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Calendar.** A meeting held every 30 days first falls on April 12. April has 30 days, so working modulo 30: 12 + 30 = 42 ≡ 12 (mod 30). After one full cycle the meeting lands on the 12th again — **May 12**.\n\n## Properties of congruence\n\n- **Reflexive:** a ≡ a (mod m), because m ∣ 0. Example: 12 ≡ 12 (mod 5).\n- **Symmetric:** if a ≡ b (mod m), then b ≡ a (mod m), because m ∣ (a − b) means m ∣ −(a − b) = (b − a). Example: 17 ≡ 5 (mod 6), so 5 ≡ 17 (mod 6).\n- **Transitive:** if a ≡ b and b ≡ c (mod m), then a ≡ c (mod m), because (a − c) = (a − b) + (b − c) is a sum of multiples of m. Example: 20 ≡ 8 and 8 ≡ 2 (mod 6), so 20 ≡ 2 (mod 6).\n\n## Modular arithmetic\n\nIf a ≡ b (mod m) and c ≡ d (mod m), then\n\n- a + c ≡ b + d (mod m)\n- a − c ≡ b − d (mod m)\n- ac ≡ bd (mod m)\n\nSo you can **reduce large numbers to their remainders first**, then calculate.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Find the remainder when 2,370 + 489 is divided by 11.\n>\n> 2,370 ≡ 5 (mod 11) and 489 ≡ 5 (mod 11)\n>\n> 2,370 + 489 ≡ 5 + 5 = 10 (mod 11). The remainder is **10**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Find the remainder when (487 · 23) − 152 is divided by 9.\n>\n> 487 ≡ 1, 23 ≡ 5, 152 ≡ 8 (mod 9)\n>\n> (487 · 23) − 152 ≡ (1 · 5) − 8 = −3 (mod 9)\n>\n> A negative residue becomes the least non-negative one by adding the modulus: −3 + 9 = 6. The remainder is **6**.\n\n## Powers by repeated squaring\n\nTo find a large power modulo m, square repeatedly and **reduce after every step**, so the numbers never grow.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Compute 3⁴ (mod 5).\n>\n> 3² = 9 ≡ 4 (mod 5)\n>\n> 3⁴ = (3²)² ≡ 4² = 16 ≡ **1** (mod 5)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Compute 2²⁰ (mod 7).\n>\n> 2² = 4, 2⁴ ≡ 4² = 16 ≡ 2, 2⁸ ≡ 2² = 4, 2¹⁶ ≡ 4² = 16 ≡ 2 (mod 7)\n>\n> 20 = 16 + 4, so 2²⁰ = 2¹⁶ · 2⁴ ≡ 2 · 2 = **4** (mod 7)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The **units digit** of a number is its remainder mod 10. The units digits of 3, 3², 3³, 3⁴, … repeat 3, 9, 7, 1 — so 3²⁰⁰ ends in **1**, since 200 is a multiple of 4.\n\n## Watch: modular arithmetic\n\n![Modular arithmetic: basics and beyond](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NFg6cw7vMuU)\n\n*Video: “Modular Arithmetic: basic and beyond” by Mathunlocked, on YouTube.*",
          "objectives": [
            "Define congruence modulo m: a ≡ b (mod m) exactly when m ∣ (a − b)",
            "Model clocks, days of the week and calendars with congruences",
            "Name and justify the reflexive, symmetric and transitive properties of congruence",
            "Add, subtract, multiply and raise to powers modulo m"
          ],
