        {
          "id": "16b85a93-de08-547e-bde8-8406745719ca",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Solving linear congruences",
          "summary": "When ax ≡ b (mod m) is solvable, modular inverses, and the reduction method.",
          "body": "## What is a linear congruence?\n\nAn equation of the form **ax ≡ b (mod m)**, where x is unknown.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** You buy candies in packs of 9 (x packs) and repack them into groups of 6, with 2 candies left over: **9x ≡ 2 (mod 6)**.\n>\n> 3x − 8 ≡ 10 (mod 11) is not yet in standard form. Add 8 to both sides: **3x ≡ 18 (mod 11)**.\n\n## Does a solution exist? How many?\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Let d = gcd(a, m). Then ax ≡ b (mod m) has a solution **if and only if d ∣ b**, and in that case it has exactly **d incongruent solutions** modulo m.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **No solution.** 9x ≡ 2 (mod 6): gcd(9, 6) = 3 and 3 ∤ 2, so there is **no solution** — the candy story is impossible.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Two solutions.** 6x ≡ 4 (mod 8): gcd(6, 8) = 2 and 2 ∣ 4, so there are 2 solutions. Test every residue:\n\n| x | 6x | 6x mod 8 | Equal to 4? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | No |\n| 1 | 6 | 6 | No |\n| 2 | 12 | 4 | **Yes** |\n| 3 | 18 | 2 | No |\n| 4 | 24 | 0 | No |\n| 5 | 30 | 6 | No |\n| 6 | 36 | 4 | **Yes** |\n| 7 | 42 | 2 | No |\n\nThe solutions are **x ≡ 2, 6 (mod 8)**. Trial and error works for small moduli, but not for large ones — so we use inverses and reduction.\n\n## The modular inverse\n\nThe **inverse of a modulo m**, written a⁻¹, is an integer with **a · a⁻¹ ≡ 1 (mod m)**. It exists **only when gcd(a, m) = 1**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **By trial.** Find 3⁻¹ (mod 5): 3 × 1 = 3, 3 × 2 = 6 ≡ **1**. So 3⁻¹ ≡ 2 (mod 5).\n>\n> **By the extended Euclidean algorithm:**\n>\n> 5 = 3(1) + 2\n>\n> 3 = 2(1) + 1\n>\n> 2 = 1(2) + 0\n>\n> Back-substitute: 1 = 3 − 2 = 3 − (5 − 3) = 3(2) − 5(1).\n>\n> In the form 1 = ax + my: 1 = 3(2) + 5(−1). The coefficient of 3 is the inverse: **3⁻¹ ≡ 2 (mod 5)**.\n\n## Method 1: multiply by the inverse (when gcd(a, m) = 1)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Solve 17x ≡ 5 (mod 43).\n>\n> **Step 1 — Euclidean algorithm:**\n>\n> 43 = 17(2) + 9\n>\n> 17 = 9(1) + 8\n>\n> 9 = 8(1) + 1\n>\n> 8 = 1(8) + 0\n>\n> **Step 2 — back-substitute:**\n>\n> 1 = 9 − 8 = 9 − (17 − 9) = 9(2) − 17\n>\n> = [43 − 17(2)](2) − 17 = 43(2) − 17(5)\n>\n> So 1 = 17(−5) + 43(2), and **17⁻¹ ≡ −5 (mod 43)**.\n>\n> **Step 3 — multiply both sides by the inverse:** x ≡ (−5)(5) = −25 ≡ **18 (mod 43)**.\n>\n> **Check:** 17 × 18 = 306 = 43 × 7 + 5, so 306 ≡ 5 (mod 43). ✓\n\n## Method 2: reduction (when d = gcd(a, m) > 1 and d ∣ b)\n\n1. Check that d ∣ b.\n2. Divide a, b **and** m by d.\n3. Solve the reduced congruence, getting x₀.\n4. List all d solutions modulo the **original** m: x = x₀ + k(m ÷ d) for k = 0, 1, …, d − 1.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** Solve 6x ≡ 8 (mod 14).\n>\n> **Step 1:** gcd(6, 14) = 2 and 2 ∣ 8, so there are 2 solutions.\n>\n> **Step 2:** divide by 2: **3x ≡ 4 (mod 7)**.\n>\n> **Step 3:** 7 = 3(2) + 1, so 1 = 7(1) − 3(2) = 3(−2) + 7(1). Then 3⁻¹ ≡ −2 ≡ 5 (mod 7), and x ≡ 5 × 4 = 20 ≡ 6 (mod 7).\n>\n> **Step 4:** k = 0 gives x = 6; k = 1 gives x = 6 + 7 = 13.\n>\n> **Answer: x ≡ 6, 13 (mod 14).** Check: 6 × 6 = 36 ≡ 8 and 6 × 13 = 78 ≡ 8 (mod 14). ✓\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Keep the **original** modulus in the final answer, and keep adding the **reduced** modulus until you have d solutions.\n\n## Watch: solving linear congruences\n\n![Solving linear congruences](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ViqgSWoSxN8)\n\n*Video: “Solving Linear Congruences, Modular Arithmetic” by Andrew Borne, on YouTube.*\n\n![Linear congruence example](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HEAokut4F4I)\n\n*Video: “Number Theory | Linear Congruence Example 2” by Michael Penn, on YouTube.*",
