    {
      "id": "c151d6c1-01c6-52dd-b8af-1feda6d66e51",
      "title": "Unit 4: Networks and Graphs",
      "description": "Model networks as graphs, then plan routes with Euler and Hamilton, build spanning trees with BFS and DFS, and find shortest paths with Dijkstra and Floyd-Warshall.",
      "activities": [
        {
          "id": "a4eaa6d0-b7e0-5861-9c6d-c26b6ccb658c",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Fundamental concepts of graph theory",
          "summary": "Vertices, edges, degree, adjacency and the main types of graphs, with real networks.",
          "body": "## Points and connections\n\nLook at a jeepney route map, a group chat, or the power lines of a barangay. Each one is made of **things** (stops, people, posts) and **connections** between them (roads, friendships, wires). Mathematicians call this structure a **graph**.\n\n## What is a graph?\n\nA graph is written as an ordered pair\n\n**G = (V, E)**\n\nwhere **V** is the set of **vertices** (points, also called nodes) and **E** is the set of **edges** (connections, also called arcs when they have a direction).\n\n- An edge between A and B is written **AB** (two-way).\n- A one-way connection from E to D is written **E→D** (an arc).\n\n## Adjacency and degree\n\nTwo vertices are **adjacent** if an edge joins them. The **degree** of a vertex is the number of edges that touch it (a loop counts twice).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Handshake check.** Every edge has two ends, so the degrees always add up to **2 × (number of edges)**. If your degree list doesn’t, you missed or double-counted an edge.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Worked example.** V = {A, B, C, D, E} and E = {AB, AC, BC, CE, E→D, B→D}. Here four connections are two-way and two (E→D and B→D) are one-way.\n>\n> | Vertex | Degree | Adjacent to |\n> | --- | --- | --- |\n> | A | 2 | B, C |\n> | B | 3 | A, C, D |\n> | C | 3 | A, B, E |\n> | D | 2 | B, E |\n> | E | 2 | C, D |\n>\n> Check: 2 + 3 + 3 + 2 + 2 = 12 = 2 × 6 edges. ✔\n\n## In-degree and out-degree\n\nWhen connections have a direction, we also count **in-degree** (arcs pointing **into** a vertex) and **out-degree** (arcs pointing **out of** it). In the example, D has in-degree 2 (from E and from B) and out-degree 0: you can reach D, but you can’t leave it along an arc.\n\n## Types of graphs\n\n| Type | What it means | Real-life example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Simple | No loops, no two edges joining the same pair | A friendship network |\n| Directed (digraph) | Every edge has a direction | One-way streets, following someone online |\n| Mixed | Some edges two-way, some one-way | A city map with two-way and one-way roads |\n| Complete (Kₙ) | Every pair of distinct vertices is joined | 4 friends who are all friends with each other |\n| Bipartite | Vertices split into two groups; edges only go between the groups | “Boys vs. girls” passing game; players vs. teams |\n| Connected | There is a route between every pair of vertices | A road network with no isolated barangay |\n| Path | Vertices in a line, each joined to the next | Stops along a single jeepney route |\n| Cycle | A closed loop; every vertex has degree 2 | A circular bus loop |\n| Tree | Connected, with no cycle (n vertices, n − 1 edges) | A family tree, water pipes from one tank |\n| Regular | Every vertex has the same degree | A cycle (all degree 2); K₄ (all degree 3) |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Which type?**\n>\n> - Four students who are all friends with one another → **complete graph** K₄.\n> - A network of one-way streets → **directed graph**.\n> - Passing the ball only to a member of the other team → **bipartite graph**.\n> - Some roads two-way, some one-way → **mixed graph**.\n\n## Modelling a real network: AirLink Philippines\n\nAirLink serves Manila (M), Cebu (C), Davao (D), Basco (B) and Puerto Princesa (P). There are two-way flights M–C and C–D, a one-way flight D→M and a one-way flight M→B. Puerto Princesa has no flight yet.\n\n- Graph: V = {M, C, D, B, P}, edges {MC, CD}, arcs {D→M, M→B}.\n- It is **mixed** (both kinds of connection).\n- It is **not connected**: P has degree 0, so no route reaches it — even though the other four towns are linked.\n- D has in-degree 0 and out-degree 1 (only D→M is an arc at D; CD is two-way).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A graph is connected only if **every** vertex can be reached. One isolated vertex is enough to make it disconnected.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Introduction to graph theory](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aj55yJ9Gz8Y)\n\n![Types of graphs](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dxhFQyivdUs)\n\n*Video suggested in the DepEd Learning Activity Sheets for Finite Mathematics 2, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- In a complete graph on 5 vertices, what is the degree of each vertex? (4)\n- A tree has 7 vertices. How many edges? (6)\n- Why does every graph have an even total degree?",
          "objectives": [
            "Define a graph G = (V, E) and identify its vertices and edges",
            "Find the degree of a vertex and the vertices adjacent to it",
