        {
          "id": "db8b87ec-96ed-5ab5-b009-e798dab52d7c",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Eulerian paths and circuits",
          "summary": "Königsberg’s bridges, Euler’s theorem and Fleury’s procedure: covering every edge exactly once.",
          "body": "## The seven bridges of Königsberg\n\nIn the 1700s, the city of Königsberg had four land areas joined by seven bridges. People asked: can you take a walk that crosses **every bridge exactly once**? In 1736, Leonhard Euler showed it is impossible — and invented graph theory doing it.\n\nMake each land area a vertex and each bridge an edge. The four land areas have degrees **5, 3, 3 and 3**. All four are odd, and (as you’ll see) that settles it.\n\n## Eulerian paths and circuits\n\n- An **Eulerian path** is a route that uses **every edge exactly once**. It may start and end at different vertices, and it may pass through a vertex many times.\n- An **Eulerian circuit** is an Eulerian path that **ends where it started**.\n\n## Euler’s theorem\n\nFor a **connected** graph, count the vertices of **odd degree**:\n\n| Odd-degree vertices | What exists |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0 | An Eulerian circuit (start anywhere) |\n| 2 | An Eulerian path, not a circuit — it must start at one odd vertex and end at the other |\n| 4, 6, 8, … | Neither |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Why?** Each time a route passes through a vertex it uses two edges — one in, one out. So every vertex in the middle of the route needs an even degree. Only the start and the end can be odd.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> A graph can never have exactly **one** odd vertex: the total degree is always even, so odd vertices come in pairs.\n\n## Fleury’s procedure\n\n1. For a circuit, start anywhere. For a path, start at one of the two odd vertices.\n2. Choose an edge leaving the current vertex — but **don’t cross a bridge** (an edge whose removal cuts the remaining edges into two separate pieces) unless it is the only choice.\n3. Travel along that edge and erase it.\n4. Repeat until every edge is used.\n\n## Worked examples\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — a path.** E = {AB, AE, BE, BC, CD, DE}.\n>\n> Degrees: A 2, B 3, C 2, D 2, E 3. Exactly two odd vertices (B and E), so there is an Eulerian path from B to E but no circuit.\n>\n> Route: **B → A → E → B → C → D → E** (6 edges, each once).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — a path.** E = {AE, ED, DF, EF, EB, BF, FC}.\n>\n> Degrees: A 1, B 2, C 1, D 2, E 4, F 4. Odd vertices: A and C.\n>\n> Route: **A → E → D → F → E → B → F → C**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — a circuit.** A pentagon A–B–C–D–E–A: every vertex has degree 2, so an Eulerian circuit exists: **A → B → C → D → E → A**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 4 — neither.** A square A–B–C–D–A with two extra “tails” A–E and C–F.\n>\n> Degrees: A 3, B 2, C 3, D 2, E 1, F 1 → four odd vertices, so no Eulerian path and no circuit.\n>\n> **Repair it:** add one edge between two odd vertices, e.g. **EF**. Now only A and C are odd, so an Eulerian path from A to C exists. Each new edge changes the degree of two vertices by one.\n\n## Where it’s used\n\nStreet sweepers, garbage trucks, mail carriers and meter readers want to cover **every street** without repeating — an Eulerian problem.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Euler paths and circuits](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5M-m62qTR-s&t=138s)\n\n*Video suggested in the DepEd Learning Activity Sheets for Finite Mathematics 2, on YouTube.*\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Always check that the graph is **connected** first. Euler’s theorem only applies to connected graphs.",
