        {
          "id": "5b61870a-5eff-55b2-be76-eb9d1cfecac9",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Hamiltonian paths and circuits",
          "summary": "Visiting every vertex exactly once, Dirac’s condition, and telling Euler and Hamilton apart.",
          "body": "## Visit every place exactly once\n\nA delivery rider leaves the depot, must stop at every customer exactly once, and come back. A tour guide wants to visit every landmark in Intramuros once. These problems are about **vertices**, not edges.\n\n## Hamiltonian paths and circuits\n\n- A **Hamiltonian path** visits **every vertex exactly once**.\n- A **Hamiltonian circuit** visits every vertex exactly once and **returns to the start**. On n vertices it uses exactly n edges.\n\n| | Eulerian | Hamiltonian |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Must use… | every **edge** once | every **vertex** once |\n| Simple test? | Yes: count odd degrees (if and only if) | No general test is known |\n| Typical problem | Sweep every street | Visit every customer |\n\n## Dirac’s condition\n\nThere is no quick “if and only if” test for Hamiltonian circuits. But there is a useful **sufficient** condition:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Dirac’s theorem.** If a simple graph has n ≥ 3 vertices and **every vertex has degree at least n/2**, then it has a Hamiltonian circuit.\n\n- Condition met → a Hamiltonian circuit is **guaranteed**.\n- Condition not met → **we can’t tell** from Dirac alone. The graph may still have one; try to build it by hand.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 1 — Dirac applies.** V = {A, B, C, D, E}, E = {AB, AC, AE, BC, BD, CD, CE, DE}.\n>\n> n = 5, so n/2 = 2.5. Degrees: A 3, B 3, C 4, D 3, E 3 — all at least 2.5. A Hamiltonian circuit is guaranteed. One is **A → B → C → D → E → A**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 2 — Dirac fails, but a circuit exists.** A hexagon A–B–C–D–E–F–A with two diagonals AC and DF.\n>\n> n = 6, so n/2 = 3. Degrees: A 3, B 2, C 3, D 3, E 2, F 3. B and E are below 3, so Dirac doesn’t apply. Yet the outer loop **A → B → C → D → E → F → A** is a Hamiltonian circuit.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example 3 — a path but no circuit.** E = {AB, AD, BC, BD, CD, DE}.\n>\n> E has degree 1, so any route that reaches E is stuck there: no Hamiltonian circuit. A Hamiltonian path starts (or ends) at E: **E → D → A → B → C**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A vertex of degree 1 can never be on a Hamiltonian circuit: a circuit needs two different edges at every vertex — one to arrive, one to leave.\n\n## Euler or Hamilton?\n\n- Mail carrier walking every street once → **Eulerian** (edges).\n- Tour guide visiting every landmark once → **Hamiltonian** (vertices).\n- Inspector crossing every bridge once → **Eulerian**.\n- Delivery driver visiting five stops once and returning → **Hamiltonian circuit**.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Hamiltonian paths and circuits](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AamHZhAmR7o)\n\n![Hamiltonian circuits examples](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OGh5JKso0y4)\n\n*Video suggested in the DepEd Learning Activity Sheets for Finite Mathematics 2, on YouTube.*",
          "objectives": [
            "Define Hamiltonian paths and circuits",
            "Apply Dirac’s condition to confirm a Hamiltonian circuit",
            "Trace Hamiltonian paths and circuits in a connected graph",
            "Differentiate Eulerian (every edge) from Hamiltonian (every vertex) problems"
          ],
          "difficulty": 3,
          "estimatedMinutes": 20,
          "maxAttempts": null,
          "passingScore": 0,
          "feedbackMode": "immediate",
          "allowSkills": false,
          "boss": null,
          "prerequisiteIds": [
            "f17c3f73-389f-5ff3-b770-ed3f111a9b71"
          ],
          "questions": []
        },
