        {
          "id": "81f963bb-670b-5ec2-b7d7-482549160e79",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Spanning trees with BFS and DFS",
          "summary": "Trees, spanning trees, and building them level by level (BFS) or deep first (DFS).",
          "body": "## Connected, without the extras\n\nA barangay wants to connect six sitios with power lines, using as few lines as possible but leaving no sitio cut off. The answer is a **spanning tree**.\n\n## Trees and spanning trees\n\n- A **tree** is a connected graph with **no cycle**. A tree on n vertices always has exactly **n − 1 edges**.\n- A **spanning tree** of a connected graph is a tree that uses **every vertex** of the graph and only some of its edges.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> A spanning tree is the minimal backbone of a network. Remove any one of its edges and the network splits in two. Add any other edge of the graph and you create a cycle.\n\n## The sitio network\n\nV = {A, B, C, D, E, F}, E = {AB, AC, BC, BD, CE, DE, DF, EF} (8 edges). A spanning tree keeps all 6 sitios connected with only 6 − 1 = **5** lines.\n\n| Vertex | Neighbours (alphabetical) |\n| --- | --- |\n| A | B, C |\n| B | A, C, D |\n| C | A, B, E |\n| D | B, E, F |\n| E | C, D, F |\n| F | D, E |\n\n## Breadth-First Search (BFS)\n\nBFS explores **level by level** using a **queue** (first in, first out). Rule: when a vertex has several unvisited neighbours, take them in alphabetical order. The edge that first reaches each vertex becomes a tree edge.\n\n1. Visit A. Queue: A.\n2. Take A out; its unvisited neighbours are B and C. Add **AB, AC**. Queue: B, C.\n3. Take B out; unvisited neighbour D. Add **BD**. Queue: C, D.\n4. Take C out; unvisited neighbour E. Add **CE**. Queue: D, E.\n5. Take D out; unvisited neighbour F. Add **DF**. Queue: E, F.\n6. Take E, then F — nothing new. The queue is empty.\n\n**BFS order:** A, B, C, D, E, F. **BFS tree:** AB, AC, BD, CE, DF.\n\n## Depth-First Search (DFS)\n\nDFS goes **as deep as possible** first, using a **stack** — think of it as the trail you walk back along. From the current vertex, move to its first unvisited neighbour (alphabetically). When stuck, back up and try the next neighbour.\n\n1. Visit A, go deeper to B. Add **AB**.\n2. From B, go to C (A is visited). Add **BC**.\n3. From C, go to E. Add **CE**.\n4. From E, go to D. Add **DE**.\n5. From D, go to F. Add **DF**.\n6. F has no unvisited neighbour; back up to A. Done.\n\n**DFS order:** A, B, C, E, D, F. **DFS tree:** AB, BC, CE, DE, DF.\n\n## Comparing the two\n\n| | BFS | DFS |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Explores | level by level | one deep branch first |\n| Uses | a queue | a stack (backtracking) |\n| Tree shape | short and bushy | long and thin |\n| Sitio tree | AB, AC, BD, CE, DF | AB, BC, CE, DE, DF |\n\nBoth trees have 5 edges and reach all 6 sitios — they just choose different edges. In BFS, each vertex is reached in order of its distance (number of edges) from the root.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A **disconnected** graph has no spanning tree: no set of its edges can reach every vertex.\n\n## Watch\n\n![BFS Breadth First Search](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/umHJzlKFGlU)\n\n*Video: “BFS Breadth First Search” by @boraxalgo, a YouTube Short suggested in the DepEd LAS.*",
          "objectives": [
            "Define trees and spanning trees and explain why they matter in network design",
            "Apply Breadth-First Search (BFS) to find a spanning tree",
            "Apply Depth-First Search (DFS) to find a spanning tree"
          ],
          "difficulty": 3,
          "estimatedMinutes": 25,
          "maxAttempts": null,
