        {
          "id": "210b5c23-33c3-5ba3-a859-cc7bd0114175",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
          "title": "Shortest paths: Dijkstra and Floyd-Warshall",
          "summary": "Weighted graphs and two algorithms for the cheapest route — one source, or every pair.",
          "body": "## Weighted graphs\n\nOn a road map, edges carry numbers: kilometres, minutes or pesos. A **weighted graph** has a number (its **weight**) on each edge. The **length** of a path is the **sum of its edge weights** — not the number of edges.\n\nThe **shortest-path problem** has two forms:\n\n- **Single-source:** the shortest path from one vertex to every other vertex.\n- **All-pairs:** the shortest path between every pair of vertices.\n\n## Dijkstra’s algorithm (single-source)\n\nWorks when **no weight is negative**.\n\n1. Give the source distance 0 and every other vertex ∞.\n2. **Finalize** the unfinalized vertex with the smallest tentative distance.\n3. For each of its neighbours, if going through it is shorter, lower the neighbour’s distance.\n4. Repeat until every vertex is finalized.\n\n**Example.** Source A. Edges and weights:\n\n| Edge | AB | AC | BC | BD | CD | CE | DE |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Weight | 2 | 5 | 1 | 7 | 3 | 8 | 2 |\n\n| Step | Finalize | A | B | C | D | E |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Start | — | 0 | ∞ | ∞ | ∞ | ∞ |\n| 1 | A (0) | **0** | 2 | 5 | ∞ | ∞ |\n| 2 | B (2) | | **2** | min(5, 2 + 1) = 3 | 2 + 7 = 9 | ∞ |\n| 3 | C (3) | | | **3** | min(9, 3 + 3) = 6 | 3 + 8 = 11 |\n| 4 | D (6) | | | | **6** | min(11, 6 + 2) = 8 |\n| 5 | E (8) | | | | | **8** |\n\n**Shortest distances from A:** B = 2, C = 3, D = 6, E = 8. The shortest-path tree uses AB, BC, CD, DE.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The direct edge A–C weighs 5, but A → B → C costs only 3. The shortest path is not always the one with the fewest edges.\n\n## Floyd-Warshall algorithm (all pairs)\n\nWorks with negative weights too, as long as there is **no negative cycle**.\n\n1. Write the **distance matrix**: 0 on the diagonal, the edge weight where an edge exists, ∞ where none does.\n2. For each vertex k in turn, check every pair i, j: is i → k → j shorter than the current entry? If so, replace it.\n3. After every vertex has served as k, the matrix holds every shortest distance.\n\n**Example.** A four-vertex loop: AB = 3, BC = 1, CD = 2, AD = 7.\n\n**Initial matrix**\n\n| | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 0 | 3 | ∞ | 7 |\n| B | 3 | 0 | 1 | ∞ |\n| C | ∞ | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| D | 7 | ∞ | 2 | 0 |\n\n- Through A: B → D becomes 3 + 7 = 10.\n- Through B: A → C becomes 3 + 1 = 4.\n- Through C: A → D becomes 4 + 2 = 6; B → D becomes 1 + 2 = 3.\n- Through D: nothing improves.\n\n**Final matrix**\n\n| | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 0 | 3 | 4 | 6 |\n| B | 3 | 0 | 1 | 3 |\n| C | 4 | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| D | 6 | 3 | 2 | 0 |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The matrix stores distances only. To recover the actual route, keep a companion “next vertex” table while updating, then follow it from the start.\n\n## Comparing the two\n\n| | Dijkstra | Floyd-Warshall |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Finds | one source to all | every pair |\n| Edge weights | non-negative only | general, no negative cycle |\n| Best when | one starting point | all pairs needed often |\n| Output | a distance list | a distance matrix |\n\nIf you need only one starting point, a single Dijkstra run is lighter. A transit app that answers “how far from station X to station Y?” many times can run Floyd-Warshall once and just look up the matrix.\n\n## Watch\n\n![Dijkstra’s Algorithm in 60 Seconds](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/8kucL88Ci8E)\n\n*Video: “Dijkstra’s Algorithm in 60 Seconds” by Web Dev Simplified, a YouTube Short suggested in the DepEd LAS.*",
          "objectives": [
            "Define a weighted graph, the length of a path and the shortest-path problem",
            "Apply Dijkstra’s algorithm to find shortest paths from one source",
            "Apply the Floyd-Warshall algorithm to find shortest paths between all pairs",
            "Compare Dijkstra’s and Floyd-Warshall algorithms"
          ],
          "difficulty": 4,
